From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rpcrt4: asynchronous remote procedures, server side, abort and cancel (3311)

CORE review: RPC (DECISIONS-2 RPC-NDR).

NdrAsyncServerCall dispatched the manager routine but nothing sent the
response: the runtime answered at once with the request's length, and
RpcAsyncCompleteCall only marshalled into a buffer.  Now an asynchronous
call goes on after the manager routine returns (the call has its copy of the
message and the request's buffer, and a reference to the connection) and
RpcAsyncCompleteCall sends the response (I_RpcSend on a server binding),
RpcAsyncAbortCall / I_RpcAsyncAbortCall send a fault, and an exception of the
manager routine before the call ended ends it with a fault.
NdrServerCallAll dispatches to NdrAsyncServerCall for procedures that are
RPCF_Asynchronous, to NdrServerCall2 for the others.

Client: RpcAsyncCancelCall(TRUE) abandons the call (notification, status and
RpcAsyncCompleteCall give RPC_S_CALL_CANCELLED; the connection is dropped),
RpcAsyncCancelCall(FALSE) sends a cancel PDU (PKT_CO_CANCEL, now a known
packet type) which the server sees through RpcServerTestCancel (now
implemented; RpcAsyncGetCallHandle gives the handle to test).
RpcAsyncCompleteCall of a call which is not complete is
RPC_S_ASYNC_CALL_PENDING.  Notifications are made from a copy of the state,
also when the connection fails or the call is cancelled.

Fixes found on the way: the async state was not zeroed (garbage flags);
a TCP connection's wait for data returned at a stale event before any data
had arrived (the notification came early and RpcAsyncCancelCall could not
cancel); a header-only packet made the receiver wait for the next packet.
---

